{"schema":1,"slug":"wyandot-ohio-detroit-and-kentucky-raids","nation":"Wyandot","baseRef":"/data/web/territories/_base-ohio-valley.json","yearStart":1745,"yearEnd":1795,"presenceLabel":"Wyandot town, raid or hunting area here","presenceNote":"Wyandot towns were north of the Ohio (Sandusky River, Detroit). The Kentucky link is raids (1780, 1782) and hunting or travel on the Warriors Path; no Wyandot camp in Kentucky is documented. The start year c.1745 is approximate: the Wyandotte Nation dates the move south from Detroit to “within a few years” of 1701, and Wikipedia gives Upper Sandusky as c.1745. The Kentucky raid story is shown for 1780–1782. The place-name story has no dates. The tint shows a general area drawn from whole counties, not a boundary.","confidence":"low-medium","approximate":true,"overlapNote":"Shared Ohio country with Delaware (Fort McIntosh 1785 gave the land to both), Shawnee (Wyandot claimed the Shawnee lands west of the Miami in the 1789 Fort Harmar record: 'the Shawanese are only living upon it by their permission'), Mingo and Odawa. In Kentucky the same hunting grounds were used by Shawnee, Delaware, Mingo, Miami (Henderson 1992) and claimed by Shawnee, Cherokee and, by sale, Six Nations.","basis":"Treaty text (Fort McIntosh 1785, Greenville 1795), Wyandotte Nation history page, Wikipedia for the 1780/1782 raids, USFS appendix for the name. Town points from Wikipedia coordinates.","basisChip":"treaty + ethnohistory","timeline":{"min":1745,"max":1796},"fadeYears":0,"fadeSouth":false,"view":{"full":"428 0 540 622","narrow":"428 0 540 622","card":"428 0 540 622"},"mapLabelPos":[-83.2,41.6],"pins":[],"startEra":{"wyandot-on-kentuckys-northern-frontier":"raids","wyandot-name-kah-ten-tah-teh-fair-land-of-tomorrow":"name"},"stories":{"wyandot-on-kentuckys-northern-frontier":{"yearStart":1780,"yearEnd":1782,"presenceLabel":"Wyandot raids on Kentucky stations","approxStart":false},"wyandot-name-kah-ten-tah-teh-fair-land-of-tomorrow":{"tagOnly":true,"presenceLabel":"Place name, no mapped territory","presenceNote":"This story is about a place name, not a territory: it has no dates and no mapped boundary. The Wyandot themselves lived north of the Ohio (Sandusky River, Detroit); their link to Kentucky was hunting and travel on the Warriors Path, and raids in 1780 and 1782 (see the Wyandot raids story)."}},"legend":[{"kind":"soft","label":"Approximate general area of Wyandot towns (whole counties, not a boundary)"},{"kind":"hatch","label":"Fort McIntosh allotment (1785) or Greenville cession (1795), simplified"},{"kind":"point","label":"Site (exact position)"},{"kind":"ring","label":"Site, location approximate"}],"eras":[{"id":"homes","label":"c.1745–1785 · Sandusky and Detroit towns","short":"c.1745","yearStart":1745,"yearEnd":1779,"confidence":"low-medium","sources":["wyn","wp_wy"],"storySlugs":[],"text":"Wyandot (Wendat/Tionontati/Wenro refugees) settled near Detroit from 1701 and sent a branch south to the Sandusky River (Upper Sandusky) in the 1730s–40s. Their towns were north of the Ohio. The tint is a general area built from whole counties (the Sandusky River country and the Detroit area): approximate, not a boundary. Detroit and Sandusky points are approximate.","layers":[{"feature":"wy_area","style":"soft"},{"feature":"upper_sandusky","style":"point"},{"feature":"detroit","style":"point"}],"mapLabel":"WYANDOT","apprTag":"Approximate general area","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · c.1745–1779","mapLabelPos":[-83.2,41.6]},{"id":"raids","label":"1780–1782 · Raids on Kentucky stations","short":"1780","yearStart":1780,"yearEnd":1784,"confidence":"medium","sources":["wp_wy","wp_bryan","kyh_bryan","wp_bird","ekh591"],"storySlugs":["wyandot-on-kentuckys-northern-frontier"],"text":"Wyandot warriors took part in the June 1780 attacks on Ruddle's and Martin's Stations, then in the 15-17 Aug 1782 siege of Bryan Station beside Lenape, Shawnee and Caldwell's Rangers, and in the ambush at Blue Licks on 19 Aug. They came from their towns on the Sandusky River and near Detroit (the tinted general area, approximate). The Kentucky points mark events, not Wyandot camps. No Kentucky camp site is documented.","layers":[{"feature":"wy_area","style":"soft"},{"feature":"bryan_station","style":"point"},{"feature":"blue_licks","style":"point"},{"feature":"ruddles_martins","style":"point"},{"feature":"upper_sandusky","style":"point"},{"feature":"detroit","style":"point"}],"mapLabel":"WYANDOT","notDrawn":{"map":"WYANDOT CAMPS IN KENTUCKY: SITES UNKNOWN · NOT DRAWN","tag":"Kentucky camp sites: unknown, not drawn"},"apprTag":"Approximate general area · raid sites are events","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · 1780–1784","mapLabelPos":[-83.2,41.6]},{"id":"mcintosh","label":"1785 · Fort McIntosh allotment","short":"1785","yearStart":1785,"yearEnd":1794,"confidence":"medium","sources":["mcintosh","wyn"],"storySlugs":[],"text":"The 21 Jan 1785 Fort McIntosh treaty allotted the lands inside its line to the Wyandot and Delaware “to live and to hunt on”, with Ottawa already living there, and made them give up land east, south and west: about one third of Ohio. The hatched counties are an approximation north of the legs of the line the treaty text lets us place; the west and south closure is ambiguous in the text. Approximate, not a boundary.","layers":[{"feature":"wy_area","style":"soft"},{"feature":"mcintosh_area","style":"hatch"}],"mapLabel":"WYANDOT","apprTag":"Approximate area · county approximation of the allotment","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · 1785–1794","mapLabelPos":[-83.2,41.6]},{"id":"greenville","label":"1795 · Greenville","short":"1795","yearStart":1795,"yearEnd":1795,"confidence":"medium","sources":["greenville","wyn"],"storySlugs":[],"text":"At Greenville (3 Aug 1795) the Wyandot, with Delaware, Shawnee, Miami and others, ceded land east and south of the Greenville line (hatched, simplified; the line ends on the Ohio opposite the mouth of the Kentucky River). The tinted general area of the Wyandot towns lay north and west of that line.","layers":[{"feature":"greenville_ceded","style":"hatch"},{"feature":"wy_area","style":"soft"}],"mapLabel":"WYANDOT","apprTag":"Approximate area · simplified cession","mapSubLabel":"approximate general area · 1795","mapLabelPos":[-83.2,41.6]},{"id":"name","label":"Undated · Kah-ten-tah-teh (place name)","short":"Place name","confidence":"low","sources":["usfs_a"],"storySlugs":["wyandot-name-kah-ten-tah-teh-fair-land-of-tomorrow"],"text":"The U.S. Forest Service history appendix records that Wyandots who often came south of the Ohio to hunt or to travel the Warriors Path called the country Kah-ten-tah-teh, “Fair Land of Tomorrow”. A name is not a territory: this page has no boundary and no dates, and nothing is drawn on the map.","layers":[],"notDrawn":{"map":"PLACE-NAME STORY · NO MAPPED TERRITORY","tag":"Place name, no mapped territory"},"noBoundary":true}],"sources":[{"id":"mcintosh","label":"Treaty of Fort McIntosh, 21 Jan 1785, transcription (Wyandot Heritage Digital Archive)","url":"https://wyandotte-archive.org/digital-heritage/treaty-wyandot-etc-1785"},{"id":"greenville","label":"Treaty of Greenville, 3 Aug 1795 (Avalon Project)","url":"https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/greenvil.asp"},{"id":"wyn","label":"History, Wyandotte Nation","url":"https://wyandotte-nation.org/culture/our-history/"},{"id":"wp_wy","label":"Wyandot people (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wyandot_people"},{"id":"wp_bryan","label":"Bryan Station (Wikipedia; siege 15-17 Aug 1782)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryan_Station"},{"id":"kyh_bryan","label":"Bryan's Station marker #21, Kentucky Historical Society","url":"https://history.ky.gov/markers/bryans-station"},{"id":"wp_bird","label":"Bird's invasion of Kentucky (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird%27s_invasion_of_Kentucky"},{"id":"ekh591","label":"British, Indian Raid (ExploreKYHistory #591)","url":"https://explorekyhistory.ky.gov/items/show/591"},{"id":"usfs_a","label":"Appendix A, Daniel Boone NF history (USFS; Kah-ten-tah-teh, Kain-tuck-ee)","url":"https://www.npshistory.com/publications/usfs/region/8/daniel-boone/history/appa.htm"}],"mainMap":{"general":"mm_general","area":"mm_area","hatch":true,"points":"mm_points","labels":[{"text":"Wyandot: approximate general area c.1745–1795 (Sandusky River and Detroit); Kentucky only as 1780 and 1782 raid sites. Low–medium confidence","at":[-83.4,41.65]}]},"geojson":{"type":"FeatureCollection","features":[{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"upper_sandusky","note":"Upper Sandusky, Wyandot Co., Ohio (Wikipedia 40.8275, -83.2814). Wyandotte Nation: part of the tribe settled Upper Sandusky after Cadillac's 1701 invitation to Detroit. A BGSU thesis places the 1780s 'Sandusky Old Town' about five miles north of modern Upper Sandusky and moved in April 1782 to Crane Township; point is the modern city, good to a few miles.","approximate":true,"label":"Upper Sandusky (approximate)","dx":9,"dy":5,"anchor":"start","name":"Upper Sandusky (approximate)"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-83.2814,40.8275]}},{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"detroit","note":"Detroit (Wikipedia 42.3317, -83.0459). Wyandotte Nation: Cadillac invited the Wandat to settle near Fort Detroit in 1701; some stayed around Detroit while others went south to Sandusky.","approximate":true,"label":"Detroit","dx":9,"dy":5,"anchor":"start","name":"Detroit"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-83.0459,42.3317]}},{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"bryan_station","note":"Siege of Bryan Station, Fayette Co., 15-17 Aug 1782 (Wikipedia: Wyandot, Lenape, Shawnee with Butler's Rangers under Caldwell and Girty). Coordinates 38.0759, -84.4153 from the marker listing and the marker position.","approximate":false,"label":"Bryan Station (1782 siege, event)","dx":-9,"dy":14,"anchor":"end","name":"Bryan Station (1782 siege, event)"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-84.4153,38.0759]}},{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"blue_licks","note":"Battle of Blue Licks, Nicholas Co., 19 Aug 1782 (three days after the siege was lifted). Coordinates 38.4344, -83.9915 from the marker position (exact).","approximate":false,"label":"Blue Licks (1782, event)","dx":9,"dy":5,"anchor":"start","name":"Blue Licks (1782, event)"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-83.9915,38.4344]}},{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"ruddles_martins","note":"Ruddle's and Martin's Stations, June 1780: Wikipedia (Wyandot people) says Wyandot warriors with Girty took part. Martin's Station pin 38.2626, -84.2938 (Bourbon Co., marker position); Ruddle's has no sourced coordinate.","approximate":true,"label":"Martin’s Station (1780, event)","dx":-9,"dy":-5,"anchor":"end","name":"Martin’s Station (1780, event)"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-84.2938,38.2626]}},{"type":"Feature","properties":{"id":"extent","kind":"main-map extent","approximate":true,"note":"Main-map extent: general areas (whole-county shapes, approximate), solid lines in Kentucky and sourced points."},"geometry":{"type":"GeometryCollection","geometries":[{"type":"MultiPolygon","coordinates":[[[[-83.5159,40.8181],[-83.4203,40.9925],[-83.4159,41.6189],[-83.1039,41.6136],[-83.0001,41.5386],[-82.9344,41.5144],[-82.875,41.5298],[-82.8568,41.5487],[-82.8595,41.5764],[-82.8341,41.5876],[-82.7858,41.541],[-82.7179,41.5419],[-82.7137,41.5015],[-82.6159,41.428],[-82.4994,41.3816],[-82.3481,41.4284],[-82.3365,41.0658],[-82.4352,41.0645],[-82.4329,40.9929],[-82.7236,40.9945],[-82.7272,40.7112],[-82.8554,40.7051],[-82.8598,40.6464],[-82.9567,40.644],[-82.9584,40.4907],[-83.0208,40.4338],[-83.2486,40.444],[-83.2515,40.5069],[-83.4184,40.5052],[-83.4199,40.6848],[-83.4958,40.7016],[-83.5159,40.8181]]],[[[-82.7358,41.601],[-82.7188,41.6196],[-82.6778,41.618],[-82.6887,41.5859],[-82.7358,41.601]]],[[[-82.8347,41.6293],[-82.8341,41.6571],[-82.7931,41.6647],[-82.8347,41.6293]]],[[[-82.8061,41.67